Electron microscopy characterizations were conducted to study the nanostructure of NiCoFe-MOF- $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ -1h. As shown in the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figure S5) and HAADF-STEM image (Figure 2a, 2b and Figure S6), numerous homo-dispersed nanoparticles emerged after 1h pyrolysis at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The nanoparticles did not show characteristic surface-enriched distribution features (as indicated by the red dotted line in Figure S5a), suggesting the partial decomposition occurred at both the surface and the internal part of MOF-74. We then increase the magnification of electron microscopy to observe the individual nanoparticle. As can be seen from the high resolution HAADF-STEM image (Figure 2c), the individual nanoparticle possessed several distinct crystal structures. Corresponding scanning transmission electron microscope electron energy loss spectroscopy (STEM-EELS) elemental mapping (Figure) indicated the Ni, Co, Fe elements were hierarchically distributed in the nanoparticle, where Ni and Co enriched in the core and Fe distributed on the skin coat of the nanoparticle. Notably, the distribution of O perfectly matched that of Fe, suggesting a metallic NiCo alloy core and Fe oxides thin shell in the nanoparticle.
Figures $2\mathrm{e}{\mathrm{-}}2\mathrm{g}$ display the enlarged images from the selected areas marked by the rectangles in Figure 2. As shown in Figure 2e, the core nanocrystal gave clear crystal lattice fringes with a $\textrm{d}$ -spacing of $0.177~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which corresponded to the (002) plane of the NiCo alloy. The shells showed spinel structured $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ on the surface of the fcc NiCo alloy (Figures 2f and $2\mathrm{g})$ ). The d-spacing of $0.481~\mathrm{nm}$ in Figure 2f and 0.415 nm in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ corresponded to the (11-1) and (002) plane of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ . Image simulation and atomic model of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ along the [110] and [123] directions were overlapped on the experimental images. Through comparing the high resolution HAADF-STEM images with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ structure models, we found perfectly match between them (also see Figure S7 and S8), which indicated the successful fabrication of $\mathsf{N i C o/F e_{3}O_{4}}$ heterostructure within MOF-74 by partially pyrolyzing NiCoFe-MOF-74 at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1h under inert atmosphere. This unusual elemental distribution indicated the Ni, Co, Fe were dissociated with DOT ligands in the order of Ni, Co, and Fe under heating. As evidenced in thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of mono metal (Ni, Co, Fe) MOF-74 (Figure S9), the decomposition temperatures increased in the order of Ni, Co, and Fe, which reflected the bonding strengths of these three metals with the O atoms of organic ligands $(\mathrm{Ni}{-}0<\mathrm{C}0{-}0$ <Fe-O). The Ni-O and Co-O bonding were weaker than Fe-O, so that Ni and Co firstly decomposed and enriched in the core while Fe deposited as a skin layer on the surface of the nanoparticle.
EXAFS and XPS analysis of NiCoFe-MOF-74 and NiCo $/\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{MOF}{-74}$ . The bonding evolution during pyrolysis of NiCoFe-MOF-74 was also examined by X-ray absorption fine structures (Figure 3a-3c, Figure S10-12 and Table S3) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure 3d-3f). The Fourier transform extended $\mathtt{X}$ -ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) of the Ni K-edge (Figure 3a) showed the main peak $(1.56\ \textup{\AA})$ from the Ni-O contribution of NiCoFe-MOF-74 almost disappeared after heating at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1h. Contrarily, the peak of metallic Ni-Ni (or Ni-Co) contribution which located at about $2.18\mathrm{~\AA~}$ emerged as the main peak, implying most of the Ni nodes in NiCoFe-MOF-74 were decomposed to form metal nanocrystals. Interestingly, for the FT-EXAFS of Co K-edge (Figure 3b), the peak from Co-O contribution evolved into two major peaks that attributed to Co-O and Co-Co (or Co-Ni) scattering, indicating the Co-O bonds in NiCoFe-MOF-74 partially decomposed and a portion of metallic Co were formed. Lastly, as can be seen from the FT-EXAFS of Fe K edge (Figure 3c), the peak from Fe-O contribution remained as the major peak after pyrolysis, indicating most of the Fe in MOF-74 was not decomposed. The XPS spectra measurements showed consistent structural changes. $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ peak of NiCoFe-MOF-74 at 856.1 eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ mostly evolved into a new spin–orbit splitting peak at Ni (0) 852.8 eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ after 1h pyrolysis (Figure 3d). For Co $2\mathrm{p}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}$ of XPS spectra, it is observed a part of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ were reduced to Co (0) and the rest stayed bivalent, and most Fe remained bivalent in MOF-74 substrates (Figures 3e and 3f).
Oxygen evolution reaction test of NiCoFe-MOF-74, NiCo $/\mathrm{Fe_{3}}{\bf0_{4}}/{\bf M O F}{\bf-}74$ and other MOF derivatives. The electrocatalytic performance of the as-obtained samples for OER were tested using a typical three-electrode system in 1.0 $\mathsf{M K O H}$ aqueous electrolyte. The samples were deposited onto glassy carbon (GC) electrodes as the working electrodes. It can be seen from the polarization curves that NiCoFe-MOF-74 with elemental ratios of ${\mathrm{Ni~}}:{\mathrm{Co~}}:{\mathrm{Fe~}}=1{\mathrm{~}}:2{\mathrm{~}}:1$ exhibited a overpotential of $270~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , surpassing those of mono metal (Ni, Co, Fe) MOF-74 and bimetal MOF-74 (NiCo, NiCo, CoFe) (Figure S13 and S14). However, the stability of NiCoFe-MOF-74 was not satisfactory. The current density rapidly decayed to $84.7\%$ of initial value after only $^\mathrm{~8~h~}$ chronoamperometric test (Figure S15).
MOF-74 and its derivatives pyrolyzed at various temperatures for different length of time exhibited distinct OER activities. The NiCoFe-MOF- $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot1\mathrm{h}$ $(\mathrm{NiCo}/\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}74)$ catalyst showed the best performance, with an anode current density of $10\:\:\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ at an overpotential of $238~\mathrm{mV}.$ , which was substantially lower than those of the commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst $(310~\mathrm{mV})$ , the pristine NiCoFe-MOF-74 $(270\ \mathrm{~\mV})$ , and most so-far reported non-precious metal catalysts (Table S1). The MOF derivatives after $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ pyrolysis $(\mathrm{NiCoFe-MOF-74-600^{\circ}C-2h})$ , whose crystalline MOF structure were completely decomposed (Figure S3 and S4), exhibited significantly decreased OER activity with a large overpotential of $366~\mathrm{{mV}\:(j=10~\mathrm{{mA}/\mathrm{{cm}^{2})}}}$ . This result implied the dramatic decrease of surface area and the wreck of well-defined MOF pore structures in the totally decomposed MOF-74 derivative would embed a large amount of active metal sites and leaded to much decayed catalytic activity. On the contrary, the undecomposed high-specific-area MOF-74 structure in NiCoFe-MOF-74- $400^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ -1h can homogeneously dispersed the active nanoparticles within MOF-74 and provided 1-dimensional channels for facile mass transfer, which contributed to the enhanced activity of partial pyrolyzed sample.
The catalytic kinetics of NiCoFe-MOF- $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot1\mathrm{h}$ for oxygen evolution was examined by Tafel plots. A smaller Tafel slope indicates more favorable reaction kinetics in the OER process.37 Figure 4c presented the Tafel plots of electrocatalyst samples. The Tafel slope of $\mathsf{M O F-400^{\circ}C^{\circ}-1h}$ $(\mathsf{N i C o}/\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathsf{M O F}\mathrm{-}74)$ for OER was $29\mathrm{~mV/dec}$ , which was much smaller than that of pristine MOF-74 (89 mV/dec). The Faradaic efficiency of the NiCoFe-MOF-74-400oC-1h electrocatalyst was measured by a fluorescence-based oxygen sensor in a gas-tight electrochemical cell. The $0_{2}$ was produced in the electrochemical cell under a fixed OER current of $10\ \mathrm{mA}$ . The theoretical yield of oxygen evolution was calculated by assuming that all the total charge was from 4e− oxidation of water by Faraday’s law. Comparing the experimental data with the theoretical data (Figure S16), the amount of oxygen evolved corresponded to a Faradaic yield of ${>}98\%$ .
High durability of electrocatalyst is also crucial for energy conversion systems. The chronoamperometric curve at 1.47 V (vs reversible hydrogen electrode) revealed that the MOF- $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot1\mathrm{h}$ $(\mathsf{N i C o}/\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathsf{M O F}\mathrm{-}74)$ catalyst can maintain excellent catalytic stability during long-term test (Figure 4d). The hybrid structure retained $99.1\%$ of its initial OER activity after $36\mathrm{~h~}$ test, while the current loss of pristine MOF-74 and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ electrode under the same condition were as high as $15.6~\%$ and $43.2~\%$ after only $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ electrocatalysis. HAADF-STEM and XPS characterizations of $_{\mathrm{NiCoFe-MOF-74-400^{o}C-1h}}$ was conducted on the recycled electrocatalysts to probe the structural changes after $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ durability test. As shown in the HAADF-STEM images of the NiCoFe-MOF-74-400oC-1h after electrocatalysis (Figure S17), the electrocatalyst remained the rugby ball shaped nanostructures with particles homogeneously distributed within their body. We then observed the individual nanoparticle of used electrocatalysts with high-resolution HAADF-STEM and verified the retainment of hetero NiCo/ $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanostructure (Figure S18). Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{\mathsf{i}}$ , Co $2\mathfrak{p}$ and Fe $2\mathrm{p}$ XPS spectra of ${\tt N i C o F e-M O F-74-400^{\mathrm{o}}C-1h}$ after $36~\mathrm{~h~}$ chronoamperometric I-t durability test (Figure S19) showed highly consistent peaks with those of electrocatalyst before durability test (Figure 3d-3f), further demonstrating the high structural stability of NiCoFe-MOF-74-400oC-1h for OER in 1.0 M KOH.
Density Functional Theory (DFT) Calculations of OER on NiCo/ $\bf{F e_{3}O_{4}}$ (001).  To understand the nature of the high OER activity on $\mathrm{NiCo/Fe_{3}O_{4}/M O F–74}$ , we performed DFT calculations to investigate the free energy profiles of the four-electron OER reaction mechanism. We considered the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ (001) surface models with and without NiCo substrate. The elementary electrochemical steps considered for the OER process include,
$$
