Notably, the effects of core NiCo on energetics of OER intermediates adsorbed on the shell $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ could also be contributed from the electrostatic interactions, including the tuning of charge density (oxidation states). However, this effect is insignificant based on our results of theoretical modeling: with all magnetic moments suppressed (spin polarization turned off), our calculations predict that the reaction free energies for OER on NiCo/ $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ surface differ very little (by less than $0.04~\mathrm{eV}$ from those on pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ surface, as shown in Figure S20. This strongly implies that the drastic difference between the two cases as shown in Figure 5 (and thus the boost in OER efficiency by the core NiCo) originates mainly from the magnetic interaction, i.e., the exchange interaction between the same spins in the core NiCo and the shell $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ with OER intermediates.
The synergetic effect of NiCo with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ was also demonstrated by controlled experiments. We prepared $_{\mathrm{NiCoFe-MOF-74-400^{\mathrm{o}}C-0.5h,}}$ , which possessed NiCo nanoparticles without $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ deposited on it (Figure S21) and Fe-MOF-74- $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ -1h which contained $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanocrystals (Figure S22), and compared their OER activity with NiCoFe-MOF-74- $400^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ -1h. The OER polarization curves showed the electrocatalyst with encapsulated $\mathsf{N i C o/F e_{3}O_{4}}$ heteroparticles in the substrate exhibited much enhanced activity than those with NiCo nanoalloy and those with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanocrystal (Figure S23).
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have developed a controlled pyrolysis method to prepare unique N $\mathrm{iCo}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}74$ nanocatalyst for highly efficient OER in alkaline media. The hybrid catalyst takes advantage of porous configuration of the MOF and interaction between metal/metal oxide particles arose from partial pyrolysis, and showed excellent activity and stability for OER. We believe the controlled partial pyrolysis strategy can provide a new perspective for the synthesis of MOF-derived OER catalysts, which will enrich the research scope of MOF and MOF-derived materials and help to explore more highly efficient electrocatalysts.
